hadiths[1]{hadithnumber,text}:
"2822","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Maymun al-Makki, narrated to us Sufyan from al-Thawri. Also narrated to us Bundar, narrated to us Yahya. Also narrated to us Abu Musa, narrated to us Abd al-Rahman, they both said: narrated to us Sufyan. Also narrated to us Salam ibn Junadah, narrated to us Waki' from Sufyan—this is the narration of Bundar—from Bukayr ibn 'Ata' from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ya'mar who said:\n\nI came to the Prophet (ﷺ) at 'Arafah, and some people from the people of Najd came to him while they were at 'Arafah. They asked him, and he ordered a caller to announce:\n\n*\Hajj is 'Arafah. Whoever comes on the night of Muzdalifah before the break of dawn has indeed attained Hajj. The days of Mina are three. Whoever hastens in two days"","" there is no sin upon him"","" and whoever delays"","" there is no sin upon him.\""*\n\nThen he had a man ride behind him to announce. Abu Bakr said: This wording *\""Hajj is 'Arafah\""* is of the type that I have mentioned in the Book of Faith"","" where a name in the definite form may apply to part of a thing with branches and parts. The Prophet (ﷺ) used the definite name *Hajj* to refer to 'Arafah"","" intending the standing there"","" for standing at 'Arafah is not the entirety of Hajj"","" but rather part of its components"","" not all of it. I have explained this type in the Book of Faith with sufficient detail for whoever Allah grants guidance and correctness.\n\nCHAPTER:"""""
